                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So uhhhh this is definitely a game I've played!\n[h2]Story[/h2]\nTo get it out of the way: it's okay. It's not meant to be the focus of the game so it does its job just fine.\n[h2]Gameplay[/h2]\nIt can be split in two categories:\n[h3]Free level exploration[/h3]\nThere's several small maps with collectibles scattered all around, and some people to talk to every once and again. Sometimes it spices it up with mechanics from other genres. These levels' purpose is to fill the gaps between one boss and the next. Without spoiling too much, there's some platforming and [b]a lot[/b] of timed challenges.\nMain issue with this is that the challenge in these scenarios stems exclusively from dealing with the controls. I don't mind tank controls, but there are levels here that require a level of precision that movement controls don't provide, resulting in a lot of trial and error to beat what should be really simple traversal sections. Map design is good but I found it to be at odds with the game design for this reason.\nThe collectibles mentioned at the start are a nice touch. Some require you to solve puzzles. Some of them are notes that give you a little bit of lore if you care about that, and some give you tips about the level boss encounter's mechanics. Overall I liked them.\n[h3]Boss encounters[/h3]\nThe meat of the game. At the end of every level, there's a boss fight that plays like Punch-Out. You can block, dodge to the sides, and punch left, right, upper left and upper right. Controls are simple enough and the bosses have some really cool ideas when it comes to design as to give every enemy enough variation with such a simple control scheme, as well as to make every fight stand out from each other. My main issues come from the execution of said ideas.\nLearning how to react to a lot of attacks is very much trial and error, since the cues for said attacks are for the most part exclusively visual. Sound is super underutilized here, which is a real shame since by having the whole game be black and white, and the screen becoming more and more bloated with every phase change, there's a severe lack of communication to the player as to what they're meant to do to avoid getting combo'd and lose towards the end of fights.\nThis becomes even more egregious considering how fights usually go. All of them have three phases, with the first two being simple as to showcase what the boss's basic moves and its whole gimmick are, with the addition of simple combos in the second one. So far so good! The third phase changes the background making it, in my opinion, way more difficult to understand what's going on on screen at any time. Enemy attacks are also faster while your recovery time from stronger attacks is still the same, usually meaning that failing to dodge one of those attacks in a combo string means you lose. Also combos are also longer and have faster startup animations, usually by skipping animation frames from cues you've already learned.\nOh, and every time you lose? Back to the first phase, which means you have to learn the counters and timings of all attacks and combos for each phase, without getting confused by how similar a lot of them are between each other. What this means is that trying to learn a boss's last phase consists of doing the same first two over and over again, hoping to catch a glimpse of new information that you can use the next time you get to said phase. It quickly becomes tedious and then you realize that [b]this loop applies to every single fight[/b].\n[h2]Conclusion[/h2]\nNo game in recent memory comes to mind when I think of having such a non-fun time as I did with this. There is stuff to like here, considering I did put almost 10 hours into it, but did I have fun in those 10 hours? Absolutely not. The more I played this, the less fun I had. I kept playing because I hoped it would catch a second wind and I started liking it again. Alas, no dice.\nLooks good, sounds okay, plays super clunky.\nIf you decide to give it a go, here's my two cents:\n[list]\n[*][b]Cap the framerate to 60FPS[/b]. There's a bunch of sections in the free roam levels, and also some of the gimmicks in certain bosses, that are affected by framerate. Going above this threshold makes it way more difficult to deal with said mechanics and, in my experience, nigh impossible to beat some of them. Luckily the game's artstyle allows for this to not be jarring and, in my experience, it didn't have any drawbacks. Do be aware that there's no way of capping the framerate in the options menu of the game, so you'll have to use external means[b].[/b]\n[*][b]Play with a controller[/b]. Don't know if this was a me issue, but I beat the whole game with a keyboard. I noticed the game dropped some of my inputs every now and then, but didn't think too much of it. At some point, I decided to try and play with a controller instead, and the game became much more responsive during fights. So if you're experiencing the same issue, give this a shot!\n[/list]",
